// PENDING: from an OPEN SigmaHQ pull request. Not reviewed or merged by SigmaHQ yet.
// Pull request: #6122 update: Regsvr32 detection rules tuning and expansion
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/6122
// Pull request author: swachchhanda000
// Change: update to existing rule
// Title: Potential Regsvr32 Commandline Flag Anomaly
// Sigma ID: b236190c-1c61-41e9-84b3-3fe03f6d76b0
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Florian Roth (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2019-07-13 | Modified: 2026-10-18
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1218.010
// Description: Detects regsvr32 invocations where the "/i" (DllInstall) flag is used without "/n" (skip
//   DllRegisterServer/DllUnregisterServer). Legitimate use of "/i" is normally paired with "/n"
//   since DllInstall is meant as an alternative to the standard registration path. When "/n" is
//   absent, regsvr32 both registers the DLL and runs DllInstall, which is an unusual combination
//   outside of abuse scenarios.
// False positives:
//   - Administrator typo might cause some false positives
// References:
//   - https://twitter.com/sbousseaden/status/1282441816986484737?s=12
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/fba4b94a2237d0ec8fe1cd5dc9c4c29dc6d3e4c4/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_regsvr32_flags_anomaly.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where ((FolderPath endswith "\\regsvr32.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "REGSVR32.EXE") and (ProcessCommandLine contains " -i" or ProcessCommandLine contains " /i" or ProcessCommandLine contains " –i" or ProcessCommandLine contains " —i" or ProcessCommandLine contains " ―i")) and (not(ProcessCommandLine contains " -n " or ProcessCommandLine contains " /n " or ProcessCommandLine contains " –n " or ProcessCommandLine contains " —n " or ProcessCommandLine contains " ―n "))
